INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'opm-federal-hiring-fair-29-october-20261028', 'global', 'USA',
'OPM Federal Hiring Fair on 29 October 2026: 16 Agencies, On-the-Spot Offers — but Only for Applicants Already in the Process',
'',
'The United States Office of Personnel Management will hold a virtual hiring fair on 29 October 2026, with 16 federal agencies interviewing candidates online and making offers on the spot — but only for applicants already in the federal hiring process.',
'',
$$<p>The United States Office of Personnel Management (OPM) will hold a virtual OPM federal hiring fair on Thursday 29 October 2026, with 16 federal agencies interviewing candidates online and making job offers on the spot. The catch, stated up front, is that the fair is not open to everyone: OPM has confirmed it is only for applicants who are already going through the federal hiring process, Government Executive reported the plan on 27 October 2026, after OPM Director Scott Kupor described it in a podcast interview.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance: OPM federal hiring fair</th><th>Detail</th></tr><tr><td>Event</td><td>OPM virtual hiring fair for early-career workers</td></tr><tr><td>Date</td><td>Thursday 29 October 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Format</td><td>Virtual, with online interviews</td></tr><tr><td>Agencies taking part</td><td>16 federal agencies</td></tr><tr><td>What happens</td><td>Agencies interview candidates and make offers on the spot, Kupor said</td></tr><tr><td>Who can take part</td><td>Only applicants already in the federal hiring process, OPM confirmed</td></tr><tr><td>Recruitment goal</td><td>More than 30,000 early-career employees</td></tr><tr><td>Government-wide target</td><td>At least 33% of external hires to be early-career staff in fiscal 2027</td></tr><tr><td>Agency list and positions</td><td>Not published by OPM, the Partnership said</td></tr></table>
<h2>OPM federal hiring fair: what will happen on 29 October</h2>
<p>Kupor set out the plan in plain terms. “I’ll call it a hire-palooza for lack of a better word,” he said on The Charlie Kirk Show. “We’re going to have 16 agencies who are literally going to use an online campaign and do online interviews with folks, and we’re going to make job offers on the spot on Oct. 29.” The agencies will therefore interview and decide on the same day.</p>
<p>An OPM spokesperson then clarified the scope in a statement to Government Executive. “The purpose of the hiring fair is to coordinate across agencies to complete virtual interviews and initiate job offers for early career positions across the country,” the spokesperson said. That means it is a coordination exercise for people already in the pipeline, not a fresh opening. OPM has not stated a salary range, a post count for the fair itself, or an application deadline for it, and none is given here.</p>
<h2>OPM federal hiring fair: the 30,000 target and the 33% rule</h2>
<p>OPM said the event supports its goal of recruiting more than 30,000 early-career employees into the federal workforce. Fiscal 2027 guidance released in the summer of 2026 set a government-wide target that at least 33% of external hires be early-career staff, Government Executive reported. The aim is to reverse a fall in the share of younger staff: figures from the Partnership for Public Service cited by Government Executive put the share of federal workers under 30 at 8.9% in September 2024 and 7.9% in January 2026.</p>
<p>The same report notes the recent history that makes that target harder to sell. The administration pushed out hundreds of thousands of civil servants last year as part of an effort to downsize the federal workforce, including thousands of newly hired and promoted employees who were still in their probationary periods, Government Executive reported. The Partnership attributed the fall largely to those probationary firings.</p>
<p>The fair is not OPM’s only early-career route. On Monday 26 October 2026, OPM announced a new Early Career Talent Network, launched with the White House and available at EarlyCareers.gov, meant to connect young talent with full-time federal roles, MeriTalk reported. The network builds on the US Tech Force programme, which hires early-career technologists for two-year terms, and it directs candidates to USAJOBS openings and events with federal recruiters.</p>
<h2>What OPM has not published about the fair</h2>
<p>The main gap in the announcement is practical detail. The Partnership for Public Service, argued that OPM has given too little information for potential hires to take part. “Hiring fairs can help bring early career talent into federal service, but only if participants know who is being recruited, for which positions and what’s expected of them,” Jenny Mattingley, the nonprofit’s vice president of public policy and stakeholder engagement, said in a statement to Government Executive.</p>
<p>Mattingley added that OPM has said little about why the event is limited to people already in the hiring process, which hiring authorities agencies will use, or how skills-based assessments are integrated. “Rebuilding candidates’ trust in the civil service as a stable career path after almost two years of widespread federal workforce cuts requires more transparency than what’s being provided,” she said.</p>
<h2>How to act on the OPM federal hiring fair</h2>
<ol>
<li>If you are already in the federal hiring process, check the status of your application on USAJOBS and make sure your contact details are current, so an agency can reach you before 29 October 2026.</li>
<li>Prepare for a virtual interview at short notice: keep your resume, references and any documents the agency has already asked for ready.</li>
<li>Do not wait for a public sign-up link for this fair. OPM has described it as restricted to applicants already in the process, and it has not published one.</li>
<li>If you are not yet in the process, this fair is not your entry point. Use EarlyCareers.gov to explore federal entry points and USAJOBS to search open roles and apply through the normal route instead.</li>
<li>Keep a record of every agency message about your application, including dates and the contact’s name.</li>
</ol>
<h2>A note for readers in Nepal</h2>
<p>For readers in Nepal, the limits need to be plain. The fair is for people already inside the United States federal hiring process, and is not a route to apply for a job from Nepal. No agent can sell a place at the fair or a federal job offer, and anyone asking for a fee to arrange either should not be trusted. The official channels are USAJOBS for open federal roles and EarlyCareers.gov for the early-career network. Growentix also covers legal United States work routes, including <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/farm-worker-jobs-in-usa-on-h-2a-visa-pay-housing-and-how-to-apply-20261027">farm worker jobs in the USA on the H-2A visa</a> and <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/nursing-jobs-in-usa-for-foreign-nurses-nclex-visa-and-pay-guide-20261028">nursing jobs in the USA for foreign nurses</a>. The full reporting on the fair is in <a href="https://www.govexec.com/workforce/2026/10/opm-touts-hire-palooza-event-early-career-employees/416856/">Government Executive’s report</a>, and the Early Career Talent Network is covered by <a href="https://www.meritalk.com/articles/opm-launches-early-career-talent-network-to-bolster-federal-hiring-pipeline/">MeriTalk’s report</a>.</p>$$,
